","Let's denote the time A walks as \( t \) hours.

A's speed is 10 kmph. So, the distance A covers in \( t \) hours is \( 10t \) km.

B starts 3 hours after A, so B walks for \( t - 3 \) hours.

B's speed is 20 kmph. So, the distance B runs in \( t - 3 \) hours is \( 20(t - 3) \) km.

B runs up to A when they have traveled the same distance. Therefore, we can set up the equation:

\[
10t = 20(t - 3)
\]

Now, let's solve for \( t \):

\[
10t = 20t - 60
\]

\[
10t = 60
\]

\[
t = 6 \text{ hours}
\]

Now that we have the time \( t = 6 \) hours, we can find the distance from the starting point when B runs up to A. We can use either A's or B's distance formula.

Using A's distance:

\[
\text{Distance} = 10 \times t = 10 \times 6 = 60 \text{ km}
\]

Using B's distance:

B walks for \( t - 3 = 6 - 3 = 3 \) hours.

\[
\text{Distance} = 20 \times (t - 3) = 20 \times 3 = 60 \text{ km}
\]

Both methods give the same result.

Therefore, B will cover up with A 60 km from the starting point.",relevance,"# Task Description
