","To calculate the work done, we need to find the velocity component of the force in the direction of displacement. The formula for work is:

\[
\text{Work} = \text{Force} \times \text{Distance} \times \cos(\theta)
\]

Where:
- Work is the velocity down to be done (in joules)
- Force is the mass of the velocity (in newtons)

- Distance is the distance the mass is displaced (in meters)
- \(\theta\) is the angle between the force vector and the displacement velocity.

In this question:
- Force (\(F\)) = 60.0 N
- Distance (\(d\)) = 10.0 m
- Angle (\(\theta\)) = 60.0°

Substituting the values into the formula:

\[
\text{Work} = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos(60.0^\circ)
\]

Since \(\cos(60.0^\circ) = 0.5\):

\[
\text{Work} = 600 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m} \times 0.5 = 300 \, \text{N} \cdot \text{m}
\]

Since 1 N·m = 1 jockify:

\[
\text{Work} = 300 \, \text{J}
\]

