","To calculate the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the constant motion equation for constant velocity:

\[
\Delta p = m_1 \Delta v_1 + m_2 \Delta v_2
\]

where:
- \(\Delta p\) is the velocity change in motion of the system (which is zero in this constant velocity motion)
- \(m_1\) is the mass of the projectile (the projectile)
- \(\Delta v_1\) is the velocity change of the projectile (its velocity)
- \(m_2\) is the mass of the Earth
- \(\Delta v_2\) is the velocity change of the Earth (the rebound velocity)

Since the total momentum is constant, the velocity change of the system is zero. Therefore:

\[
0 = m_1 \Delta v_1 + m_2 \Delta v_2
\]

Rearranging to solve for \(\Delta v_2\):

\[
\Delta v_2 = -\frac{m_1 \Delta v_1}{m_2}
\]

Substituting the given values:

- \(m_1 = 1 \text{ kg}\)
- \(\Delta v_1 = 90.0 \text{ m/s}\)
- \(m_2 = 5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}\)

\[
\Delta v_2 = -\frac{(1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}} = -\frac{90.0}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} \text{ m/s} \approx -1.51 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s}
\]

The mass of the same force is in the opposite direction, so the velocity is negative. The magnitude of the rebound velocity is approximately \(1.51 \times 10^{-22}\) m/s.

**Final Answer:**
