","To find the curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system, we use the formula:
\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = P(x, y)\mathbf{i} + Q(x, y)\mathbf{j} + R(x, y)\mathbf{k}
\]
\[
\mathbf{C} = \nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial R}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{i} - \left(\frac{\partial R}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{j} + \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf{k}
\]
Given that \(\mathbf{V}\) only depends on \(x\) and \(y\), we can set \(R = 0\). Thus, the vector field is:
\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = P(x, y)\mathbf{i} + Q(x, y)\mathbf{j} + 0\mathbf{k}
\]
Substituting into the curl formula:
\[
\mathbf{C} = \nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{i} - \left(\frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\right)\mathbf{V}
\]
Since \(R = 0\) and \(\mathbf{V}\) only satisfies \(x\) and \(y\), the \(z\) components are zero. Thus, \(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} = 0\) and \(\frac{\partial P}{\partial z} = 0\). Substituting these into the formula:
\[
\mathbf{C} = \left(\frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - 0\right)\mathbf{i} - \left(0 - 0\right)\mathbf{j} + \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf{k}
\]
Simplifying, we get:
\[
\mathbf{C} = 0\mathbf{i} - 0\mathbf{j} + \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf{k}
\]
\[
\mathbf{C} = \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf{k}
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is:
\[
\boxed{\mathbf{C} = \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}\right)\mathbf{k}}
\]",relevance,"# Task Description
